15833 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Has Bitcoin ever been hacked\077 Looking only at news headlines\054 it is easy to believe so\056 In 2013\054 as Bitcoin\047s price fluctuated following the Mt\056 Gox security incident amid market overheating\054 CNBC ran a headline that plainly stated\054 \042Bitcoin Hacked\042\0561\056 Reading just the title\054 one might assume the Bitcoin protocol itself had been breached\056 If the 2013 incident was a one\055off security failure\054 the 2014 Mt\056 Gox crisis was closer to a total collapse\056 At the time\054 Mt\056 Gox was the world\047s largest Bitcoin exchange\054 and approximately 850\054000 BTC\024including customer assets\024vanished\0562\056 This amounted to around \044480 million at the time\054 representing roughly 7\045 of the total circulating supply\056 While many reports accurately described it as an \042exchange bankruptcy\042 or \042exchange hack\054\042 the public simply internalized it as a \042Bitcoin hack\042\056 This narrative repeated itself in 2016 when another major exchange\054 Bitfinex\054 saw roughly 120\054000 BTC stolen\0563\056\n\nHowever\054 what was breached in these incidents was not the Bitcoin network\054 but the servers of centralized exchanges\056 Exchanges are private companies that pool and store users\047 bitcoins on centralized servers\056 To use an analogy\054 a depository holding gold belonging to multiple people was robbed\073 the fundamental properties of gold itself were not compromised\056 Hackers exploited vulnerabilities in corporate security systems\024specifically server management and internal controls\024not Bitcoin\047s cryptographic architecture\056\n\nThe Bitcoin network itself has not been hacked a single time since its launch on January 3\054 2009\0564\056 It is worth reflecting on what this means\056 For over 16 years\054 the Bitcoin network has generated a block every 10 minutes without fail\056 The value of the assets secured on this network has reached a market capitalization of approximately \0441\0566 trillion \050as of May 2026\051\056 For anyone capable of breaking this network\047s cryptography\054 the potential reward would be immense\056 Despite huge incentives for countless attackers\024ranging from independent hackers and research institutions to state\055level actors\024the system has never been breached\056\n\nBut does this robustness have an expiration date\077 This is a valid question because Bitcoin\047s defenses rest entirely on a single premise\072 that the mathematics underpinning its security remain unbroken\056 If that math is broken\054 a 16\055year flawless track record offers no guarantees\056\n\nThis report examines that premise in light of the emergence of quantum computing\056 We will sequentially explore the threat quantum computers pose to Bitcoin\047s cryptographic systems\054 how close this threat is to reality\054 and how the Bitcoin community is preparing for it\056\n\nTo state the conclusion upfront\072 quantum computing is not exclusively a Bitcoin problem\054 but a challenge for our entire modern digital civilization\056 Furthermore\054 Bitcoin already possesses the structural pathways necessary to address this challenge\056)
     /K [ 1 ]
     /P 15834 0 R
     /Pg 1310 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

