15858 0 obj
  << /ActualText (In traditional finance\054 ownership relies on third\055party verification and record\055keeping\056 Bank ledgers for deposits\054 registries for real estate\054 and depository or account records for stocks serve as the ultimate arbiters\056 The authority to determine \042who the owner is\042 is concentrated in specific institutions\056 In contrast\054 Bitcoin transactions are executed without institutional identity verification\054 seals\054 or paperwork\056 This is because control over a Bitcoin asset is established through cryptographic proof rather than human identity\056 What the network verifies is not \042who this person is\054\042 but \042can they produce a valid signature with the private key\077\042\n\nBitcoin\047s ownership system is based on the mathematical principles of public\055key cryptography\056 A user first determines a private key and mathematically derives a public key from it\056 This relationship is often compared to a lock and key\056 The private key is the key accessible only to the owner\056 This key is a 256\055bit number\054 and the number of possible combinations is approximately 2\262uv\054 or roughly 1\05615\32710ww\056 This figure is large enough to rival estimates of the total number of atoms in the observable universe\056 Such an astronomical number of possibilities means that guessing someone else\047s private key through brute force is practically impossible\056 The public key is mathematically derived from this private key and acts as the lock\056 Anyone can see this lock\054 but seeing it         \176)
     /K [ 8 ]
     /P 15859 0 R
     /Pg 1688 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

