15867 0 obj
  << /ActualText (The property that it is easy to calculate a public key from a private key\054 but practically impossible to do the reverse\054 is specifically implemented on a mathematical framework known as \042elliptic curve cryptography\042\056 Since its inception\054 Bitcoin has used the Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm \050ECDSA\051 operating on a specific elliptic curve called secp256k1\0566\056 The secp256k1 elliptic curve has a fixed point known as the generator point\056 In Bitcoin\054 a public key is created by applying a private key\054 which is a number\054 to this generator point\056 Mathematically\054 this is the result of repeatedly applying the elliptic curve\047s addition rules to the generator point as many times as the value of the private key\054 and the resulting point on the curve becomes the public key\056 This computation is performed very quickly\056 However\054 reverse\055engineering how many operations were applied\024that is\054 figuring out what the private key is\024just by looking at the resulting public key is practically impossible for today\047s classical computers\056 This is known as the Elliptic Curve Discrete Logarithm Problem \050ECDLP\051\056 As long as the computational difficulty of the ECDLP holds\054 it remains practically impossible for an attacker to recover the private key or forge a valid signature\054 even if the public key is exposed\056 This is the first mathematical pillar supporting Bitcoin\047s protection of control\056)
     /K [ 2 ]
     /P 15868 0 R
     /Pg 2394 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
