15902 0 obj
  << /ActualText (period\054 but the resulting hash values are entirely different\056 Because of this property\054 hashes are widely used to verify whether original data has been tampered with\056\n\nIn Bitcoin\054 SHA\055256 serves two core roles\056 First\054 it acts as the foundation of Proof of Work \050PoW\051\056 In Bitcoin\054 the entity that will generate the next block is determined not by specific approval or authority\054 but through a computational competition to find a hash value that meets a certain difficulty condition\056 A miner bundles transaction records and various information in the block\054 then repeatedly alters adjustable values within it\024most notably an arbitrary number called a nonce\024to find a hash value satisfying the difficulty conditions demanded by the network\056 This process is closer to a brute\055force search that attempts repeatedly until a matching value emerges\054 rather than a logical deduction to find an answer\056 Therefore\054 practically the only way to increase the probability of success is to perform more hash calculations\056 Thanks to this structure\054 block generation incurs realistic electricity and equipment costs\054 and which block is accepted as legitimate is ultimately governed by the actual computational cost expended\056\n\nSecond\054 SHA\055256 supports the integrity of records\056 In a blockchain\054 each block is sequentially linked by including the hash of the previous block\056 Consequently\054 if even a single bit of a past block\047s content is altered\054 the hash of that block changes\054 which subsequently breaks the connection to all following blocks that reference that value\056 Tampering with a single past block practically means having to recreate all the blocks that follow it\056 However\054 in Bitcoin\054 this is not merely a matter of reorganizing data\056 An attacker would have to redo the Proof of Work for the altered blocks while simultaneously catching up to the accumulated computational power of the honest chain\054 which continues to extend in the meantime\056 Ultimately\054 overturning historical records requires the expenditure of astronomical computational resources and time\056 This is why Bitcoin\047s records are virtually immutable\056\n\nIt is precisely in this regard that SHA\055256 serves as the second mathematical pillar of Bitcoin security\056 If elliptic curve cryptography determines the legitimate control required to move assets\054 SHA\055256 dictates what records will remain as legitimate history\056 One upholds control\054 while the other underpins the continuity and cost structure of the record\056 Bitcoin\047s security holds only when these two pillars stand together\056)
     /K [ 0 ]
     /P 15903 0 R
     /Pg 2540 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

