15938 0 obj
  << /ActualText (The computers we use daily process information in units called bits\056 A bit can only exist in one of two states\054 0 or 1\054 and all calculations are composed of combinations of these binary states\056\n\nIn contrast\054 the fundamental unit of a quantum computer is the \042qubit\056\042 Until it is measured\054 a qubit does not settle into a definitive 0 or 1\054 but can exist as a combination of both states\056 This is called \042superposition\056\042 If a classical bit is like a coin lying heads or tails\054 a qubit is more akin to a coin spinning in the air\056 However\054 while a spinning coin\047s outcome is merely hidden\054 a qubit\047s superposition is fundamentally different in that it is a quantum state described as a combination of two states prior to measurement\056\n\nQuantum systems also exhibit a phenomenon known as \042entanglement\056\042 Entanglement occurs when two or more qubits form a single\054 combined quantum state\054 making it impossible to describe the state of one qubit independently of the others\056 In this state\054 measuring one qubit reveals a strong correlation with the measurement of the other\054 a correlation that persists even if the qubits are physically separated\056 If superposition is a concept that explains the state of an individual qubit\054 entanglement explains the phenomenon where multiple qubits form a single\054 inseparable combined state\056\n\nWhen superposition and entanglement are combined\054 quantum computers perform calculations in a fundamentally different way than classical computers\056 Parallel computing in a classical computer involves multiple processors dividing and handling different tasks simultaneously\056 A quantum computer\054 on the other hand\054 uses superposition and entanglement to configure its qubits into a single  \176)
     /K [ 7 ]
     /P 15939 0 R
     /Pg 3104 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

