15964 0 obj
  << /ActualText (sufficiently large\054 fault\055tolerant \050error\055corrected\051 quantum computer exists\054 it can solve discrete logarithm and integer factorization problems far more efficiently than a classical computer\0568\056 Bitcoin\047s digital signature scheme is based on the discrete logarithm problem over the secp256k1 elliptic curve\056 In other words\054 it relies on the premise that deriving a private key from a public key is hard\056\n\nIf a quantum computer capable of running Shor\047s Algorithm emerges\054 this premise would no longer hold\054 as calculating a private key from a public key becomes theoretically possible\056 This can be likened to a technology that can forge a key simply by looking at the lock\056 In Bitcoin\054 the authority to move an asset is proven by the fact that one knows the private key\056 If calculating the private key from the public key becomes possible\054 assets in addresses where the public key has already been exposed are at risk of being moved by an attacker acting as the legitimate owner\056 In this regard\054 Shor\047s Algorithm directly targets Bitcoin\047s first pillar\024the control protection structure based on elliptic curve cryptography\056\n\nConversely\054 Grover\047s Algorithm\054 proposed by Lov Grover in 1996\054 poses a different kind of threat to hash functions\056 Grover\047s Algorithm is a quantum algorithm that reduces the number of attempts required for a brute\055force search to roughly its square root\0569\056 When trying to find a value that satisfies a specific condition\054 if a classical computer requires N attempts\054 Grover\047s Algorithm reduces the required attempts to approximately \032N\056\n\nApplying this to SHA\055256 effectively lowers its 256\055bit security strength to around a 128\055bit level\056 This is certainly a significant weakening\056 However\054 128\055bit security remains exceptionally high\056 2\271\262x is an astronomically large number \050approximately 3\0564 \327 10\263x\051\054 and it is practically unsearchable with existing computational resources\056 The quantum threat to SHA\055256 is more accurately described as a reduction in computational difficulty rather than an immediate collapse of the hash function\056\n\nUltimately\054 the threat quantum computers pose to Bitcoin does not apply equally to its two pillars\056 SHA\055256 merely sees its search difficulty lowered by Grover\047s Algorithm\073 it is not a structure that collapses instantly\056 In contrast\054 elliptic curve cryptography is the direct target of Shor\047s Algorithm and faces a far more fundamental and direct threat\072 the ability to derive private keys from public keys if a sufficiently powerful quantum computer emerges\056\n\nTherefore\054 the core of the quantum threat lies in the vulnerability of control over addresses whose public keys have been exposed\056 However\054 this is strictly a theoretical possibility for now\056 For such an attack to actually occur\054 a large\055scale\054 fault\055tolerant quantum computer capable of running Shor\047s Algorithm is required\056 The question we must now explore is\072 exactly how close is this threat to becoming reality\077)
     /K [ 0 ]
     /P 15965 0 R
     /Pg 3642 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
