16105 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Among these\054 Google\047s Willow serves as a prime example illustrating the significance of recent advancements in quantum computing\056 According to Google\054 Willow performed a calculation in just 5 minutes that would take an estimated 10\262u years on a classical computer\056 However\054 Willow\047s significance lies not merely in computational speed\073 a more crucial achievement was demonstrating the so\055called \042below threshold\042 phenomenon in error correction experiments\054 where increasing the number of qubits actually reduced the error rate\056 This demonstrated that adding more qubits does not simply accumulate errors\073 rather\054 within an appropriate error\055correction framework\054 the stability of logical qubits can actually improve\056\n\nNevertheless\054 this achievement does not immediately translate to decryption capabilities\054 as the problem Willow solved was a benchmark designed specifically to measure quantum computer performance\056 Google also made this clear in interviews following the announcement\054 with a spokesperson stating\054 \042Current estimates suggest it will take at least another 10 years to break RSA3\051\054 and this would likely require about 4 million physical qubits4\051\056\04214\056 Thus\054 while Willow\047s performance is a major step forward showcasing the potential of quantum computers\054 it is still far from the practical ability to break cryptographic systems\056\n\nIn the case of Bitcoin\054 the scale of a quantum computer required for decryption is massive\073 researchers estimate that millions to tens of millions of physical qubits would be needed to attack the elliptic curve signature scheme Bitcoin uses\056 A research team at the University of Sussex\054 basing their resource estimates on specific error\055correction methods and hardware assumptions\054 estimated that approximately 13 million physical qubits could be required to attack Bitcoin\047s secp256k1\055based elliptic curve signature scheme within a single day\05615\056 Considering that Google\047s Willow chip is at a 105\055qubit scale\054 the gap to the required level is roughly 120\054000\055fold\056 This gap illustrates that a significant distance remains between current quantum computing technology and practical cryptographic attacks\056\n\nTo understand why the number of qubits required for decryption is so large\054 one must consider the error rate problem inherent in quantum computers\056 Because qubits rely on highly sensitive quantum states\054 they are easily prone to errors even from minor environmental interference\056 Consequently\054 performing actual calculations requires a large number of additional physical qubits dedicated to error correction\056 For this reason\054 quantum computing research distinguishes between \042physical qubits\054\042 which are actually implemented in hardware\054 and \042logical qubits\054\042 which are stable computational units constructed through error correction\056 Depending on the error\055correction scheme and the underlying error rate\054 implementing a single stable logical qubit can require hundreds to thousands of physical qubits\056 The \04213 million qubits\042 estimate provided by the University of Sussex team also refers to the scale of physical qubits\054 including those needed for error correction\054 not logical qubits\056 Furthermore\054 translating this into a physical device requires not only the qubits themselves but also a massive hardware infrastructure for control\054 measurement\054 cooling\054 and wiring\056\n\nDue to these technical limitations\054 current quantum computers are often described as being in the \042NISQ \050Noisy Intermediate\055Scale Quantum\051\042 era\056 This term\054 proposed by physicist John Preskill\054 refers to early\055stage\054 noisy quantum \056  )
     /K [ 6 ]
     /P 16106 0 R
     /Pg 5430 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

