  << /ActualText (computers possessing anywhere from 50 to a few hundred qubits\05616\056 While NISQ devices can physically realize phenomena like quantum superposition and entanglement\054 their high levels of noise and errors make it difficult to perform stable calculations over extended periods\056 Thus\054 while current quantum computers show meaningful results in specific experimental problems or limited optimization calculations\054 they are still technically ill\055equipped to handle massive computations like breaking Bitcoin\047s cryptographic system\056\n\nHowever\054 this does not mean the quantum threat can be ignored\073 the critical issue is not whether quantum computers can break Bitcoin immediately\054 but the difficulty of accurately predicting when that threat will materialize\056 Quantum computing research is advancing rapidly\054 and issues surrounding error correction and hardware scalability are gradually improving\056 Ultimately\054 the quantum threat is a matter of a timeline\054 not an imminent collapse\056 For Bitcoin to endure as a global financial infrastructure in the long run\054 it must prepare for a post\055quantum transition in advance\056 This transition is not merely about swapping out a single cryptographic algorithm\073 it is a long\055term migration process requiring wallets\054 nodes\054 exchanges\054 users\054 and protocol consensus to move in unison\056 If preparations are made early\054 the transition will be an orderly upgrade\054 but if delayed\054 it becomes a race against time to outmaneuver attackers equipped with quantum computers\056)
     /K [ 0 ]
     /P 16111 0 R
     /Pg 5708 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
