16180 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Post\055Quantum Cryptography does not refer to a single algorithm\073 rather\054 it is a collection of cryptographic techniques based on various mathematical problems that are difficult for quantum computers to solve efficiently\056 Currently\054 one of the most prominent approaches is lattice\055based cryptography\056 Lattice\055based cryptography derives its security from the computational difficulty of high\055dimensional lattice problems and is considered a strong candidate for both key establishment and digital signatures\056 However\054 it carries the burden of increased key and signature sizes\054 which can raise storage and communication costs during system implementation\056 Hash\055based signatures offer the advantage of relatively simple security assumptions and well\055studied stability over long periods\054 but they can be disadvantageous regarding signature size and computational efficiency\056 For this reason\054 future security infrastructure is highly likely to evolve by combining multiple cryptographic techniques with different mathematical foundations rather than relying on a single technology\056\n\nThe important point is that this transition is no longer confined to the research phase\056 NIST has stated that these new standards are ready for immediate use and has urged computer system administrators to begin transitioning to the new standards as soon as possible\056 Major tech companies have also begun implementing post\055quantum cryptography on an experimental or partial basis\056 Google and Cloudflare have tested and partially deployed post\055quantum key exchange methods in internet communication protocols like TLS\05418\056 and Apple introduced the PQ3 protocol to iMessage\054 which incorporates post\055quantum cryptography\05619\056 IBM is also advancing post\055quantum security infrastructure\054 including a quantum\055safe TLS mode for IBM Cloud and a PQC transition plan for the IBM Quantum Platform\05620\056 Furthermore\054 Zoom announced that it is offering post\055quantum end\055to\055end encryption \050E2EE\051 features in Zoom Workplace and Zoom Meetings\05621\056\n\nThis trend reveals an important truth\072 for now\054 the spread of quantum\055resistant cryptography is outpacing the development of quantum computers capable of real\055world attacks\056 In effect\054 the shield is being institutionalized before the spear\056 Long before quantum computers become an actual threat\054 the global internet infrastructure and financial systems have already begun migrating to new cryptographic regimes\056\n\nThis international transition holds significant implications for Bitcoin\073 if Bitcoin must transition to post\055quantum cryptography in the future\054 it is highly unlikely to be forging an entirely new path alone\054 but rather following a path the world is already taking\056 However\054 Bitcoin\047s transition is not a system update that can be centrally mandated by a government or corporation\056 It is a matter of consensus)
     /K [ 22 ]
     /P 16181 0 R
     /Pg 6534 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

