  << /ActualText (Bitcoin is commonly perceived as a system immutable by design\054 built upon unalterable rules\056 This conventional wisdom is valid to a certain extent\056 The foundational components that govern the network\047s core order\024such as the hard\055capped money supply\054 the Proof\055of\055Work \050PoW\051 mechanism\054 difficulty adjustment cycles\054 and transaction verification rules\024are notoriously resistant to change\056 However\054 this does not mean the Bitcoin protocol is entirely static\056 More precisely\054 Bitcoin is a system that \042cannot be changed arbitrarily\056\042 Alterations are entirely possible\054 but the process mandates public proposals\054 rigorous technical scrutiny\054 code implementation\054 voluntary adoption by network participants\054 and a comprehensive social consensus\056\n\nThese protocol modifications are typically debated through a standardized framework known as the Bitcoin Improvement Proposal \050BIP\051\056 A BIP serves to document modifications to the Bitcoin protocol or associated standards\054 providing a mechanism for peer\055reviewing technical specifications\054 the rationale for adoption\054 and anticipated systemic impacts\056 Proponents initially present the necessity and feasibility of their ideas in open forums\054 such as the Bitcoin Development Mailing List \050bitcoindev\051\05422\056 to gather community feedback\056\n\nOnce a draft matures\054 it is submitted as a pull request to the GitHub BIP repository\056 BIP editors review the submission against formatting and scope criteria before assigning a unique number and merging it into the repository\056 Crucially\054 being registered as a BIP does not equate to formal adoption or network consensus\056 Final activation hinges entirely on whether developers\054 full\055node operators\054 miners\054 wallet\057exchange providers\054 and end\055users actually implement and execute those rules in their software\056\n\nThe mechanisms for altering protocol rules are broadly categorized into \042softforks\042 and \042hardforks\056\042 A softfork is a backward\055compatible upgrade that introduces tighter restrictions to the existing rule set\056 Nodes that choose not to   \176)
