16232 0 obj
  << /ActualText (The initial phase of a quantum\055resistant migration does not involve the overnight deprecation of the legacy signature scheme\056 Rather\054 it requires establishing a technical gateway capable of accommodating new spending conditions within the Bitcoin protocol\056 As demonstrated by earlier upgrades such as SegWit and Taproot\054 the transition to post\055quantum cryptography will likely begin with the introduction of new output structures\054 allowing users to voluntarily migrate their assets to secure addresses over an extended period rather than immediately invalidating existing addresses and transaction models\056\n\nOne of the leading proposals currently under discussion is outlined in BIP\055360\054 which introduces a new output type known as Pay\055to\055Merkle\055Root \050P2MR\051\05626\056 Taproot outputs do not record an internal public key directly\056 Instead\054 they record an output key derived from the internal public key and script\055tree information\056 P2MR\054 by contrast\054 removes Taproot\047s key\055path spending mechanism and records only the Merkle root of a script tree containing multiple spending conditions\056 Put simply\054 rather than revealing in advance the public keys or detailed conditions required to spend an asset\054 P2MR leaves only a cryptographic commitment on the blockchain indicating that those conditions exist\056\n\nBecause P2MR records the script tree as a 256\055bit hash commitment\054 it can provide an output structure capable of accommodating future quantum\055resistant signature schemes and related script conditions should they eventually be introduced into Bitcoin\056\n\nBIP\055360 distinguishes between two categories of quantum attacks\072 long\055exposure attacks and short\055exposure attacks\056 A long\055exposure attack refers to a scenario in which a public key remains visible on the blockchain for an extended period\054 allowing an attacker to use a future quantum computer in an attempt to recover the corresponding private key\056 Structures such as Pay\055to\055Public\055Key \050P2PK\051 and Taproot\054 both of which depend on public\055key\055based spending mechanisms\054 may be relatively more vulnerable to this type of attack\056\n\nA short\055exposure attack\054 by contrast\054 involves deriving a private key from a public key that has been revealed while a transaction is sitting in the mempool and then broadcasting a competing transaction with a higher fee\056 P2MR still requires the public key associated with the active signature scheme to be revealed when funds are actually spent\056 Consequently\054 in systems such as ECDSA or Schnorr signatures\054 where public keys become visible at the time of spending\054 P2MR alone cannot prevent short\055exposure attacks that occur while a transaction remains in the mempool\056\n\nBIP\055360 is not\054 in itself\054 a proposal to introduce quantum\055resistant signature algorithms such as ML\055DSA or SLH\055DSA into Bitcoin\056 Rather\054 it is best understood as a proposal to establish an effective output structure that would    \176)
     /K [ 4 ]
     /P 16233 0 R
     /Pg 7378 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
