16241 0 obj
  << /ActualText (enable the future adoption of quantum\055resistant signature schemes\056 The BIP\055360 document itself notes that P2MR alone cannot prevent short\055exposure attacks targeting public keys that are briefly exposed in the mempool before transaction confirmation\056 It further acknowledges that more sophisticated quantum threats may ultimately require the adoption of quantum\055resistant signature schemes\056\n\nIn this sense\054 P2MR is less a complete defense than a foundation for transition\056 It preserves as much as possible of the flexible script structure provided by Taproot while reducing the problem of long\055term public\055key exposure before coins are spent\056 It also represents a conservative approach aimed at minimizing the changes required of existing infrastructure\024including wallets\054 exchanges\054 and software libraries\024by leveraging the current Taproot framework wherever possible\056\n\nThis approach\054 however\054 is not without costs\056 Because P2MR eliminates Taproot\047s key\055path spending mechanism\054 transaction data can become larger than in the simplest Taproot key\055path spends\056 A standard Taproot key\055path spend requires only a signature\054 whereas P2MR also requires the spending script and proof that the script is included in the Merkle tree\05627\056\n\nConsequently\054 P2MR reduces the risk associated with the long\055term exposure of public keys\054 but it may impose additional costs in terms of block space and transaction fees\056 This trade\055off becomes even more significant if quantum\055resistant signature algorithms are eventually introduced into Bitcoin\056 In general\054 quantum\055resistant signatures are substantially larger than ECDSA or Schnorr signatures\054 meaning that improved security may come at the cost of increased transaction size and verification overhead\056\n\nNevertheless\054 the broader significance of BIP\055360 lies in demonstrating that Bitcoin\047s transition to a quantum\055resistant system does not necessarily require a disruptive hard fork\056 By preserving existing address formats while introducing new output types\054 P2MR provides a voluntary migration path through which users can gradually move their assets to more secure addresses according to their own risk assessments\056 In this framework\054 the transition is not defined as a single mandatory event but as a gradual process in which secure pathways are established first and migration follows over time\056\n\nIn summary\054 Bitcoin\047s transition to a quantum\055resistant system is not a technological impossibility\056 The broader cybersecurity ecosystem has already entered the standardization and deployment phase of post\055quantum cryptography\056 Within Bitcoin\054 active efforts are underway to develop structures that minimize public\055key exposure and accommodate future quantum\055resistant signature schemes\056\n\nMoreover\054 a post\055quantum transition differs fundamentally from governance disputes such as changing Bitcoin\047s fixed supply cap\054 where stakeholder interests may be directly opposed\056 If quantum computing advances to the point where Bitcoin\047s signature schemes become vulnerable\054 miners\054 developers\054 exchanges\054 wallet providers\054 and investors alike would face a common systemic threat\056 Under such circumstances\054 broad consensus regarding the necessity of a transition would likely emerge relatively quickly\056\n\nThe central challenge therefore lies not in whether a transition is possible\054 but in how it is implemented\056 Success will depend on a complex combination of         \176)
