16254 0 obj
  << /ActualText (To comprehensively evaluate Bitcoin\047s post\055quantum migration process\054 one must first critically examine the dimension of the \042timeline\056\042 The threat levied against the Bitcoin network by quantum computing will not materialize as a single\054 uniform event\056 More precisely\054 the nature of the threat shifts dramatically depending on when the protocol transition is initiated\056 A scenario where Bitcoin proactively integrates defensive mechanisms prior to the commercialization of quantum attacks is fundamentally distinct from a scenario where remediation begins reactively after quantum exploits have surfaced\056 The former constitutes a manageable\054 orderly transition\054 whereas the latter escalates into a high\055stakes race against adversarial exploitation\056\n\nUnder the proactive scenario\054 the transition can be engineered as a controlled\054 non\055disruptive process that preserves network stability\056 The network can integrate post\055quantum address formats and advanced spending conditions into the protocol well in advance\054 allowing users an extensive grace period to securely relocate their assets\056 Here\054 the primary objective is ensuring procedural simplicity and accessibility\054 enabling holders to utilize their private keys to migrate assets to post\055quantum addresses\056 Users execute standard transactions via upgraded wallet or exchange interfaces\054 and network nodes validate these inputs against the updated rule set\056 Within this paradigm\054 the transition ceases to be a systemic shock and instead operates as a prolonged\054 predictable asset migration\056\n\nConversely\054 a reactive scenario introduces extreme security risks\056 If protocol upgrades are only initiated after quantum computers reach the technical threshold required to derive private keys from on\055chain public keys\054 the very act of moving legacy assets to secure post\055quantum addresses becomes a vector for exploitation\056 In a normal environment\054 a user simply broadcasts a transaction to route assets to a new address\056 However\054 in an ecosystem haunted by an active quantum adversary\054 the moment a user signs a transaction to spend from a legacy output\054 their public key is exposed to the mempool\056 A sophisticated attacker can intercept this brief exposure\054 compute the private key almost instantly\054 and broadcast a front\055running transaction with  \176)
     /K [ 4 ]
     /P 16255 0 R
     /Pg 7770 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
