16259 0 obj
  << /ActualText (a higher fee to seize the assets before the original transaction is confirmed\056\n\nAt this stage\054 the transition ceases to be an orderly software deployment\073 it becomes a race over whose transaction gets confirmed first\056 Ultimately\054 the timing of the transition is the definitive variable that dictates whether this challenge culminates in an orderly evolution or a destructive conflict\056\n\nThis precise problem statement is consistently mirrored in academic research focused on post\055quantum migration protocols\056 In a 2018 paper\054 a research team led by I\056 Stewart at Imperial College London\047s Centre for Cryptocurrency Research and Engineering proposed a transition protocol utilizing a \042Commit\055Delay\055Reveal\042 mechanism\05628\056 This architecture was specifically designed for an adversarial environment where post\055quantum signature schemes have been integrated into the Bitcoin protocol\054 yet a substantial volume of assets remains trapped in legacy\054 non\055quantum\055resistant outputs\056 True to its name\054 the protocol enforces three distinct phases\072\n\nCommit\072 In the initial phase\054 a user does not expose their legacy public key or their new post\055quantum public key directly\056 Instead\054 they publish a single cryptographic hash that binds both keys together\056 Because hash functions are resistant to reverse\055engineering\054 an adversary cannot work backward from the hash to discover the public keys\056 This hash serves as an on\055chain commitment\024a sealed promise of a future claim without revealing the underlying data\056\n\nDelay\072 Following the hash commitment\054 the user cannot immediately move the assets\056 Instead\054 they must wait for a required period \050the researchers proposed an illustrative period of six months\051\056 This mandatory delay ensures that the commitment becomes deeply embedded in the blockchain\047s history\056 If the commit were only a few blocks deep\054 a well\055resourced attacker could execute a chain reorganization to erase the record and substitute a fraudulent one\056 However\054 once the commitment is buried under months of accumulated blocks\054 the economic and computational cost of rewriting the ledger becomes prohibitively high\054 making the commitment practically irreversible\056\n\nReveal\072 After a sufficient delay period has elapsed\054 the user reveals the legacy public key\054 the new quantum\055resistant public key\054 and the verification data proving that the relationship between the two keys was previously committed\056 At the same time\054 the user submits a signature generated with the new quantum\055resistant private key to demonstrate control of the new quantum\055resistant key as well\056 Even if an attacker observes the revealed legacy public key and subsequently derives the corresponding legacy private key\054 they still do not possess the new quantum\055resistant private key\056 Nor can they retroactively create the commit record that only the legitimate owner could have established before the delay period began\056 As a result\054 under the new rules\054 learning the legacy private key after the fact is not sufficient to seize the asset\056 The authors argue that this approach can remain effective even in a scenario where ECDSA has already been compromised\056 Although it requires modifications to the Bitcoin protocol\054 they note that it could be implemented through a soft fork\056\n\nOf course\054 this architecture is not a panacea for all security blind spots\056 The       \176)
