16268 0 obj
  << /ActualText (mechanism primarily safeguards outputs whose public keys have not yet been directly exposed on the blockchain\024legacy P2PKH addresses being a prime       example\056 It cannot fully remediate legacy outputs whose public keys are already deeply woven into the blockchain\047s historical ledger\054 nor can it accommodate crisis scenarios requiring the rapid mass evacuation of assets under time constraints\056\n\nNevertheless\054 the profound takeaway from this research is clear\072 the core of the post\055quantum transition is not merely the introduction of a new cryptographic signature scheme\056 The authentic challenge lies in engineering      the institutional and technical procedures required to safely usher legacy assets into a new security paradigm\056 Deciding when public keys are revealed\054 determining the duration of commit delays\054 and establishing the validation criteria for legitimate transfers all require a fundamental rewriting of consensus rules\056 In short\054 post\055quantum migration is not an isolated swap of cryptographic algorithms\073 it is the comprehensive design of a governance architecture to relocate legacy capital into a new security order\056\n\nYet\054 regardless of how flawlessly a migration architecture is designed\054 not all on\055chain assets will participate in this migration\056 Legacy software wallets may lack the updates required to compile new address formats\054 users may remain oblivious to the urgency of the migration\054 and long\055term passive investors may consciously choose inaction\056\n\nFar more severe categories of assets exist\072 coins where private keys have been permanently lost\054 owners who have passed away\054 or accounts where physical access has been completely severed\056 These \042lost coins\042 cannot move autonomously\054 irrespective of how innovative the transition pathways are\056 Ultimately\054 technical migration vectors can only rescue assets backed by active human agency\073 they cannot inherently dissolve the systemic risks posed by stagnant\054 unmanaged assets anchored to the ledger\056\n\nFurthermore\054 relying on a hardfork\055centric overhaul does not bypass this dilemma\056 Proponents of a hardfork possess the authority to fundamentally rewrite the treatment of the existing UTXO set\056 They can theoretically design an architecture that freezes high\055risk addresses\054 imposes punitive conditions on legacy withdrawals\054 or alters asset weights during the generation of a new chain snapshot\056\n\nHowever\054 implementing heavy\055handed consensus rules from the top down does not automatically relocate unmanaged legacy assets into post\055quantum secure addresses\056 Attempting to forcefully integrate legacy coins into a post\055quantum architecture without an explicit signature from the rightful owner requires the enactment of artificial intervention rules that violate the foundational principles of a decentralized ledger\056 Thus\054 a hardfork is not a magical solution that dissolves the dilemma\073 rather\054 it is a volatile governance arena that demands the ecosystem make disruptive political judgments regarding which assets to legitimize and which to abandon\056\n\nAt this point\054 the transition to quantum\055resistant cryptography effectively divides bitcoin into two categories\072 coins that can be moved and coins that remain unmoved or cannot be moved\056 The former category is relatively straightforward\056 If owners retain their private keys\054 wallets and exchanges support the new address format\054 and sufficient time is available\054 these coins     \176)
