16277 0 obj
  << /ActualText (can be migrated to the new system\056 The challenge lies with the latter category\056 Legacy coins whose public keys have already been exposed\054 coins that have remained dormant for extended periods\054 lost coins whose private keys are no longer available\054 and assets whose owners\047 intentions cannot be verified\024such as Satoshi Nakamoto\047s early coins\024remain separate issues that must be addressed during the transition process\056\n\nConsequently\054 in Bitcoin\047s transition to a quantum\055resistant system\054 developing new signature schemes and address formats is only the first task\056 The more difficult question is how to regard coins that do not migrate to the new system\056 Are they the assets of legitimate long\055term holders\077 Are they inaccessible lost coins\077 Are they vulnerable assets that could be targeted by quantum attackers because their public keys have been exposed\077 Or are they risk\055bearing assets that the network may need to address in a different way\077 This is precisely the issue explored in the next chapter\056 The question is not whether Bitcoin can transition to a quantum\055resistant framework\054 but how to deal with coins that do not\024or cannot\024participate in that transition\056)
