16350 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Yet the dilemma extends beyond the creator\047s holdings\056 Permanently inaccessible assets\024commonly known as \042lost coins\054\042 where private keys have been lost due to negligence\054 hardware failure\054 or the owner\047s death\024present a separate challenge in a post\055quantum migration\056 On\055chain inactivity alone cannot tell us whether a coin is truly lost or simply being held long\055term\056 The real problem lies in coins whose private keys are genuinely lost or whose owners can no longer demonstrate their intent\056 Because these coins cannot migrate to a new post\055quantum address\054 they leave an unresolved question about how the network should handle them\056\n\nConsequently\054 the post\055quantum transition simultaneously exposes two distinct vectors of systemic friction\056 The first is a cryptographic vulnerability concerning historically exposed public keys\054 which become immediate targets for exploitation once sufficiently powerful quantum computing architectures emerge\056 The second is a governance dilemma regarding the treatment of stagnant or unmanageable assets\056 If a sovereign holder maintains control over their private keys\054 migration to a secure domain is a matter of voluntary execution\056 However\054 assets stripped of human agency or missing their underlying private keys are structurally precluded from participating in the upgrade\056 While these two problems frequently intersect\054 their underlying characteristics are profoundly different\072 the former is a technical problem of cryptographic vulnerability\054 whereas the latter is a political and philosophical judgment regarding ownership\054 immutability\054 and the legitimate boundaries of network intervention\056\n\nThis combination of vulnerabilities forces the Bitcoin network to interpret the true nature of motionless capital\054 transitioning the debate into a conflict between \042quantum theft\042 and \042quantum recovery\056\042 If an adversarial actor utilizes a quantum computer to expropriate an old coin with an exposed public key\054 should the decentralized ledger accept the transaction as a valid expenditure under its procedural rules\077 Alternatively\054 to preempt such exploitation\054 should the network enforce strict protocol\055level restrictions on legacy spending after a designated grace period has lapsed\077 This inquiry transcends simple security policy\073 it strikes at the core of how ownership is defined within Bitcoin\054 and the extent to which the network can legitimately manipulate established consensus rules in the name of security\056)
     /K [ 0 ]
     /P 16351 0 R
     /Pg 9454 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
