16367 0 obj
  << /ActualText (not arbitrarily block specific coins\054 and maintains the rule that \047a valid signature is a valid spend\056\047 However\054 the cost is significant\056 From the original owner\047s perspective\054 the transaction amounts to theft\056 Furthermore\054 if Satoshi coins or large long\055dormant holdings suddenly move\054 the market may interpret this as a signal that a massive potential supply has been unlocked\056 The rules are preserved\054 but the economic trust those rules were meant to protect may be shaken\056\n\nThe second paradigm is Freezing\056 This approach restricts the spendability of outputs with exposed public keys that fail to migrate to post\055quantum addresses after a designated grace period\054 effectively making certain legacy UTXOs unspendable\056 Freezing is proposed as a safeguard to prevent a quantum attacker from computing private keys and moving large amounts of assets\056 Given that the sudden movement of Satoshi\047s coins or long\055dormant outputs could trigger severe market panic even without actual selling\054 proactive freezing appears to be the most direct defensive measure\056 However\054 freezing directly conflicts with Bitcoin\047s foundational principles\056 In Bitcoin\054 control over assets is defined by possession of a private key\054 not by legal identity or the approval of a central authority\056 If the network blocks a coin from being spent simply because it has been \042dormant for a long time\042 or is \042vulnerable to quantum attack\054\042 this creates an exception to the principle that anyone who can produce a valid signature can spend the coin\056 Furthermore\054 legitimate long\055term holders\054 individuals who recovered their keys late\054 or users who missed the migration window due to technical difficulties could also find their access blocked\056 Freezing may deter attackers\054 but it carries the risk of locking out rightful owners at the same time\056\n\nThe third paradigm is Conditional Recovery\054 a middle ground between preserving existing rules and full freezing\056 Under this approach\054 once the migration grace period expires\054 coins with exposed public keys can no longer be spent using standard elliptic curve signatures alone\056 Instead\054 legitimate owners are given an alternative pathway to prove they have controlled the asset and transfer it to a post\055quantum address\056 Theoretically\054 Zero\055Knowledge Proofs \050ZKPs\051 could be used to provide a limited recovery path for certain types of owners\05630\056 A Zero\055Knowledge Proof is a cryptographic technique that allows someone to prove they know a secret without revealing the secret itself\056 It may therefore be possible to design a recovery process where an owner proves legitimate control under certain conditions without exposing any private information on the blockchain\056\n\nHowever\054 this approach must be handled with care\056 If the recovery process relies solely on proving knowledge of the private key\054 it cannot distinguish between the rightful owner and a quantum attacker who has computed the same key\056 For conditional recovery to be meaningful\054 it requires a separate secret that has not been exposed on the blockchain\024something that cannot be reverse\055engineered from the public key and is known only to the legitimate owner\056 A BIP\0553931\056 seed phrase\054 used by many modern wallets\024a sequence of words used to recover a wallet\024is one such example\056 A quantum attacker cannot derive a seed phrase from an exposed public key\056 However\054 early Bitcoin wallets did not use seed phrases\054 and there is no way to verify whether any such separate secret exists for Satoshi coins or other early mining outputs\056 This approach also offers no solution for genuinely lost coins where the private key no longer exists\056 Therefore\054 while ZKP\055based conditional recovery may         \176)
     /K [ 2 ]
