16372 0 obj
  << /ActualText (offer a viable path for some modern wallets and users\054 it cannot serve as a universal solution to the broader problem of Satoshi coins and lost coins\056\n\nThe fourth paradigm is Burning\054 a more extreme choice than freezing\056 Under this approach\054 any output with an exposed public key that fails to migrate to a post\055quantum address within the grace period is permanently made unspendable or removed from the circulating supply\056 Burning eliminates the risk of a quantum attacker seizing lost or dormant coins and introducing them to the market\056 In the long run\054 it also removes the uncertainty that quantum\055vulnerable coins could appear on the market at any time\056 However\054 the cost is the greatest of all four options\056 Burning sets a precedent that the network can arbitrarily invalidate the legitimate long\055term holdings of rightful owners\056 If one of the reasons Bitcoin is trusted is its resistance to arbitrary confiscation and censorship\054 burning is the option that conflicts most directly with that principle\056 The moment a precedent is set that part of the supply can be removed for security reasons\054 Bitcoin\047s procedural immutability can no longer carry the same meaning it once did\056\n\nUltimately\054 each of the four options carries its own risks\056 The status quo preserves procedural neutrality but accepts the risk that a quantum attacker could move old coins\056 Freezing blocks that possibility but may also restrict the spending rights of legitimate long\055term holders\056 Conditional recovery attempts a compromise but only works in limited cases where a separate proof condition can distinguish the rightful owner from a quantum attacker\056 Burning is the strongest defensive measure but does the most damage to Bitcoin\047s censorship resistance and the principle that ownership is inviolable\056\n\nTherefore\054 the problem of Satoshi coins and lost coins is not a question of which cryptographic algorithm to adopt when quantum computers arrive\056 It is a question of how the Bitcoin ecosystem chooses to interpret coins that do not move\056 Are these the legitimate assets of long\055term holders\077 Are they inaccessible lost coins\077 Are they ownerless assets that a quantum attacker could seize\077 Or are they risky assets that the network must protect or isolate\077 These questions cannot be answered by technology alone\056 They require the Bitcoin community to make a judgment about what it values most\024ownership\054 immutability\054 security\054 or economic stability\056\n\nThis is where the next chapter begins\056 If no consensus is reached on these options\054 Bitcoin may face not just a technical upgrade but a deeper conflict over the legitimacy of the chain and the meaning of its procedural immutability\056)
     /K [ 0 ]
     /P 16373 0 R
