16391 0 obj
  << /ActualText (If the four choices discussed in the previous chapter fail to reach a single consensus\054 the issue ultimately shifts to determining which rules to follow\056 In this situation\054 a hardfork is not merely an upgrade method\054 but an event where a network that shares the same history splits into chains operating under different rules\056 Looking at the exact same UTXO set\054 one chain can continue to recognize the existing signature rules as they are\054 while the other can place restrictions on spending coins that are vulnerable to quantum attacks\056 In this scenario\054 Bitcoin faces a choice between two chains with different interpretations of ownership\054 rather than a simple technical upgrade\056\n\nOne side may choose to maintain the existing rules\056 On this chain\054 as long as a valid signature is presented\054 no distinction is made between whether the signature was created by the original owner or with a private key computed by a quantum computer\056 It maintains the principle that ownership in Bitcoin is determined by a valid signature\054 not by identity or intent\056 From this perspective\054 the network\047s role is not to interpret who the owner is\054 but to verify signatures that match the rules\056\n\nConversely\054 the other chain could place separate restrictions on old coins with exposed public keys or coins that have not moved for a long time\056 It could restrict the spending of coins that do not migrate to post\055quantum addresses within a certain grace period\054 require additional conditions for coins with exposed public keys\054 or freeze or burn specific categories of UTXOs\056 This chain judges that the existing signature rules alone are no longer sufficient\056 In a situation where quantum computers undermine the cryptographic foundations\054 a valid signature alone cannot determine rightful ownership\056\n\nAt this point\054 the two chains share the same history but implement different philosophies\056 The former maintains the principle that \042a valid signature constitutes a valid spend\054\042 while the latter holds that \042under conditions where quantum attacks are possible\054 ownership cannot be judged by the existing signature rules alone\056\042 The former emphasizes procedural neutrality\054 while the latter emphasizes system protection and economic stability\056 It is difficult to say that either side is simply right or wrong\054 because both hold onto core values of Bitcoin\056\n\nA similar case already exists in cryptocurrency history\056 In 2016\054 within the Ethereum ecosystem\054 a smart contract project designed like a decentralized investment fund\054 \042The DAO\054\042 was attacked\056 The attacker exploited a                      \176)
     /K [ 5 ]
     /P 16392 0 R
     /Pg 9984 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
