16396 0 obj
  << /ActualText (vulnerability in The DAO\047s smart contract\054 rather than the Ethereum protocol itself\054 to extract a large amount of Ether\056 This raised the question of whether outcomes produced by code should be accepted as they are\054 or whether the community should intervene to reverse the damage\056 Ultimately\054 the Ethereum community chose a chain that recovered the funds through a hardfork\05632\056 This hardfork was executed at block 1\054920\054000\054 and the Ethereum Foundation explained at the time that approximately 12 million ETH were moved to a withdrawal contract\056 Some participants who opposed this maintained the existing chain\054 resulting in the formation of \042Ethereum Classic \050ETC\051\056\042 This event shows that a hardfork is not just a technical fix\054 but a question of what should be viewed as legitimate history\056\n\nThe quantum computing issue raises the same question\072 which transactions should be recognized as legitimate history\056 If a consensus cannot be reached on whether the movement of old coins with exposed public keys should be viewed as a valid spend or restricted as a quantum theft\054 Bitcoin will split into chains with different ownership philosophies\056 In this case\054 which chain is the \042legitimate Bitcoin\042 is not decided by code alone\056 Legitimacy is formed through the choices of node operators\054 miners\054 exchanges\054 wallet providers\054 custody institutions\054 institutional investors\054 self\055custody users\054 and ordinary users\024choices about which chain to mine\054 which to label as BTC\054 which to recognize as a custody asset\054 and which to hold long\055term\056 A hardfork is an event that splits code\054 but legitimacy is created through the social choices that run that code and give it value\056)
