16404 0 obj
  << /ActualText (A more radical possibility can also be considered here\056 Instead of freezing or burning old coins within the existing Bitcoin chain\054 this involves creating a chain with entirely new rules and encouraging institutional infrastructure to recognize that chain as the new standard Bitcoin chain\056 In this scenario\054 a snapshot would be taken based on a specific block height\054 and coins that do not move to post\055quantum addresses within a certain grace period would be classified as \042unmoved assets\056\042 The new chain could then be designed to allocate these coins to a network security fund\054 a development fund\054 or a public goods fund\056\n\nTechnically\054 such a fork is not impossible\056 Since a hardfork introduces new consensus rules that are incompatible with existing rules\054 the new chain can be designed to handle UTXOs that do not meet certain conditions differently from the existing chain\056 For example\054 a rule could be created where coins with exposed public keys that do not move to post\055quantum addresses by a certain point are no longer recognized as assets that individuals can spend\054 and are instead assigned to the new chain\047s public goods fund\056\n\nIn this case\054 supporters of the fork could argue that rather than leaving old\054 vulnerable coins as easy targets for quantum attackers\054 it is more rational to quarantine them on the new chain and convert them into resources for the security and development of the entire network\056 As discussed in the previous chapter\054 the possibility of Satoshi coins or long\055dormant coins moving can be perceived by the market as a potential supply shock\056 In this respect\054 a                    \176)
