16409 0 obj
  << /ActualText (redistributive hardfork is not just a technical defense\054 but a political and economic proposal to redefine the ownership and use of old coins in light of the quantum threat\056\n\nHowever\054 the core of this scenario is legitimacy\054 not technology\056 Anyone can create a forked chain with new rules\054 but no fork automatically gains Bitcoin\047s legitimacy\056 For the new chain to acquire the name and liquidity of Bitcoin\054 major exchanges\054 custody providers\054 ETF issuers\054 institutional investors\054 miners\054 node operators\054 and self\055custody users must accept that chain\056 Especially since Bitcoin has integrated deeply into institutional finance\054 which chain is recognized as the underlying asset for ETFs\054 which chain is treated as an eligible asset in custody systems\054 and which chain maintains the BTC ticker on major exchanges can heavily influence market choices\056\n\nInstitutional products like spot Bitcoin ETFs may include provisions that leave the judgment of which network to view as appropriate for trust purposes to the sponsor\047s discretion in the event of a hardfork\056 For example\054 the prospectus for BlackRock\047s iShares Bitcoin Trust ETF explains that if a hardfork occurs on the Bitcoin network\054 the sponsor can use its discretion\054 within the scope permitted by the trust agreement\054 to determine which network is appropriate for trust purposes\05633\056\n\nThis does not mean that the ETF sponsor can change the consensus rules of the Bitcoin network\056 It simply means that if multiple chains coexist due to a hardfork\054 the sponsor can decide which chain to treat as Bitcoin within the financial product it manages\056 In other words\054 institutional products can become selectors that grant a specific chain its name and liquidity in a fork scenario\054 rather than mere observers\056\n\nThis is precisely where the political and economic significance of an institutional hard fork emerges\056 If large financial firms such as BlackRock\054 major exchanges\054 custodians\054 and even regulators and governments effectively favor a particular forked chain through tax laws\054 accounting standards\054 custody rules\054 and exchange regulations\054 many institutional investors and financial products may have strong incentives to follow that chain\056 In that case\054 a hardfork is no longer merely a technical split within the community\056 Instead\054 it becomes a question of which chain institutional infrastructure chooses to recognize and support with liquidity\056 Bitcoin\047s legitimacy will be contested not only in code repositories and developer mailing lists\054 but also through ETFs\054 custodial services\054 accounting standards\054 tax rules\054 exchange listings\054 and institutional investment portfolios\056\n\nYet at that very point\054 a redistributive hardfork most directly challenges Bitcoin\047s identity\056 Just because institutional entities support a specific fork does not automatically mean there is consensus across the entire Bitcoin community\056 If self\055custody users\054 full\055node operators\054 miners\054 and the existing developer community do not accept those rules\054 the new chain may remain a separate forked chain approved by institutions rather than a continuation of Bitcoin itself\056 Institutions can move a significant portion of the name and liquidity\054 but that alone cannot completely determine Bitcoin\047s legitimacy\056\n\nA more fundamental issue is that the justification of public goods can conflict with Bitcoin\047s ownership principles\056 The idea of securing unmoved coins as public goods runs into the problem that lost coins and long\055term held coins are  \176)
     /K [ 0 ]
     /P 16410 0 R
     /Pg 10406 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
