16418 0 obj
  << /ActualText (indistinguishable on\055chain\056 Just because a coin has not moved for years or decades does not mean it is definitively a lost coin\056 It could be the asset of a long\055term holder\054 an asset waiting to be inherited\054 or an asset unmoved for political\054 legal\054 or personal reasons\056 Therefore\054 incorporating a coin into a public goods fund simply because it has not moved by a certain point requires accepting the burden of re\055judging ownership\054 going beyond a simple security measure\056\n\nWho will manage the public goods fund is also a question\056 If it is a developer fund\054 which developers will distribute those resources\077 If it is a security fund\054 which miners or validation infrastructure will receive the rewards\077 If a government manages it\054 Bitcoin risks being drawn back into the very sovereign financial system it was designed to operate outside of\056 If private financial institutions manage it\054 is it truly a public good\054 or is it a new reserve controlled by institutional custody infrastructure\077 The moment these questions are opened\054 Bitcoin shifts back to a system that requires trusted managers\056\nTherefore\054 a redistributive hardfork is both a powerful scenario for institutionally managing the risks of quantum attacks and the option that most directly tests Bitcoin\047s censorship resistance\054 the inviolability of ownership\054 supply rules\054 and the source of its legitimacy\056 It appears to be a proposal to convert lost coins into public goods\054 but in reality it raises deeper questions\072 who can judge what constitutes a lost coin\054 who can decide what Bitcoin is\054 and who holds the authority to redistribute unmoved assets\056 These questions cannot be answered by technology alone\056 Ultimately they come down to a single question\072 will Bitcoin\047s future be shaped by institutions and governments\054 or by the decentralized community of users\054 developers\054 and node operators who have always been its true foundation\077)
     /K [ 0 ]
     /P 16419 0 R
     /Pg 10530 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

