16429 0 obj
  << /ActualText (The quantum computing issue is a question that exposes Bitcoin\047s vulnerabilities while at the same time reaffirming what it stands for\056 Bitcoin\047s total supply is hard\055capped at 21 million\054 its halving schedule is predictable\054 and there is no institution\024like a central bank or government\024that can adjust monetary rules at its discretion\056 This predictability is a key reason Bitcoin has built long\055term trust\056 If the rules of a currency change easily\054 it is difficult for anyone to trust it as a long\055term store of value\056\n\nHowever\054 Bitcoin\047s immutability does not mean that every part of its code is frozen forever\056 Bitcoin has already been upgraded several times and may need to change in the future to respond to new threats\056 What matters is not whether it changes\054 but how it changes\056 Parts of the code can change in response to new threats\054 but for that change to gain legitimacy\054 it must pass through public verification\054 voluntary adoption\054 and distributed consensus\056 Bitcoin\047s immutability does not mean that nothing changes\073 it means that no one can change the rules without consensus\056\n\nIf a situation arises where the cryptographic system is genuinely compromised\054 maintaining the existing rules could instead put the entire system at risk\056 If signature rules designed to protect ownership no longer keep ownership secure\054 adjusting those rules is not an abandonment of principle\024it is a change made in service of it\056 In this respect\054 the post\055quantum transition does not conflict with Bitcoin\047s immutability\073 it is an evolution necessary to sustain it\056\n\nHowever\054 not all changes carry the same legitimacy\056 The introduction of new signature schemes and address formats can be accepted as an upgrade that strengthens security\056 But the handling of unmoved coins\054 hardforks\054 institutional chain selection\054 and attempts to convert assets into public goods raise deeper questions\056 It is not simply a matter of which technology to adopt\054 but a question of who can judge ownership\054 who can decide what Bitcoin is\054 and what kind of process makes a change legitimate\056\n\nTherefore\054 the fundamental question raised by quantum computing is whether Bitcoin can handle necessary changes without undermining its own principles\056 When change is necessary\054 it must be possible\056 But no one should be able to change it arbitrarily\056 That tension\024between the ability to adapt and the resistance to arbitrary change\024is where Bitcoin\047s future will be written\056)
     /K [ 1 ]
     /P 16430 0 R
     /Pg 10674 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

